(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-coagulation-hemostasis-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Coagulation & Hemostasis Testing Quiz 2 — Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a platelet count of 15,000 per microliter (adult reference 140,000 to 400,000). Which finding is expected?","choices":["Petechiae and spontaneous bleeding","Bleeding only after major surgery","Bruising without spontaneous bleeding","Bleeding into joints and muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Below 20,000 per microliter the patient may have spontaneous bleeding and petechiae. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has thrombocytopenia, and her smear shows fragmented red blood cells. What are these fragments called?","choices":["Schistocytes","Target cells","Spherocytes","Teardrop cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The smear in thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura shows red cell fragments, called schistocytes, in addition to the thrombocytopenia. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has mucosal bleeding and a normal platelet count. Which studies evaluate von Willebrand disease?","choices":["Prothrombin time and international normalized ratio","von Willebrand antigen, activity and factor VIII","D-dimer and fibrin monomers","Fibrinogen and thrombin time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The von Willebrand studies are von Willebrand factor antigen and activity plus a factor VIII level. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with cirrhosis has an enlarged spleen and a low platelet count. Which mechanism lowers the circulating count?","choices":["Consumption in clots","Sequestration in the spleen","Impaired marrow production","Immune destruction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypersplenism pools platelets in an enlarged spleen, so fewer circulate. Increased platelet sequestration is a cause of thrombocytopenia. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A newborn has umbilical stump bleeding and lifelong severe bleeding later. The prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time and thrombin time are normal. Which deficiency fits?","choices":["Factor VII","Factor XIII","Factor VIII","Factor IX"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Factor XIII deficiency gives umbilical stump bleeding and lifelong severe bleeding of any tissue with normal screening tests. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has von Willebrand disease. Which phase of hemostasis does the missing factor serve?","choices":["Primary hemostasis","Secondary hemostasis","Fibrinolysis","Vasoconstriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. von Willebrand factor tethers platelets to exposed subendothelial collagen, so its deficiency impairs formation of the platelet plug. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has calf cramping and a low suspicion of thrombosis. In which situation does a D-dimer add the most value?","choices":["When a normal result is expected","When a clot is already known to be present","When the clot location is needed","When platelet function is in doubt"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer is most useful for its negative predictive value: a normal result helps exclude thrombosis, so it adds value only when a normal result is plausible. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman takes warfarin, and her international normalized ratio is 4.8 (goal 2.0 to 3.0). What is the concern?","choices":["Clot risk","Platelet excess","Fibrinolysis","Bleeding risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. On warfarin a high international normalized ratio means over-anticoagulation and a risk of bleeding. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has thrombocytopenia. Which platelet index describes the uniformity of platelet size and helps with the differential diagnosis?","choices":["Mean corpuscular volume","Red cell distribution width","Mean platelet volume","Bleeding time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mean platelet volume indicates the uniformity of size of the platelet population and is used for the differential diagnosis of thrombocytopenia. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman needs a fibrinogen level measured. Which method is preferred?","choices":["A prothrombin time derived value","Bleeding time","Mean platelet volume","The Clauss assay"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Clauss assay is preferred over a prothrombin time derived fibrinogen. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a low suspicion of thrombosis, and her D-dimer is below the reference limit. What does the normal result do?","choices":["It confirms thrombosis","It shows the clotting factors are normal","It measures platelet function","It helps exclude thrombosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer is best used for its high negative predictive value: a normal result helps exclude thrombosis. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman bleeds easily, and her prothrombin time and international normalized ratio are normal. What does this mean?","choices":["A coagulopathy is not excluded","A coagulopathy is excluded","Platelet function is normal","Fibrinolysis is normal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A normal prothrombin time and international normalized ratio do not rule out coagulopathy; platelet disorders and other defects can coexist. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has a platelet count of 6,000 per microliter (adult reference 140,000 to 400,000). Which risk is highest?","choices":["Bruising only with major trauma","Excess bleeding only with surgery","Spontaneous intracranial bleeding","No added risk until the count is lower still"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Below 10,000 per microliter the patient is at risk for spontaneous intracranial bleeding and serious hemorrhage. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation. Which laboratory test monitors the therapy?","choices":["D-dimer","Platelet count","International normalized ratio","Bleeding time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Warfarin monitoring is an indication for coagulation studies, and the international normalized ratio is the test used, with a typical goal of 2.0 to 3.0. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a prothrombin time of 19 seconds (reference 11 to 13) and a normal activated partial thromboplastin time. Which deficiency fits?","choices":["Factor VII","Factor VIII","Factor IX","Factor XI"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An isolated prolonged prothrombin time points to the extrinsic pathway, factor VII deficiency. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man has a D-dimer reported. Which three enzymes act to form this cross-linked fibrin degradation product?","choices":["Thrombin, factor XIII and plasmin","Thrombin, tissue factor and factor VII","Plasmin, antithrombin and protein C","Factor Xa, factor IXa and factor VIIIa"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer results from the action of thrombin (forming fibrin), activated factor XIII (cross-linking it) and plasmin (degrading it). — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A laboratory runs a prothrombin time on a 49-year-old woman. What is added to her plasma to start the clot?","choices":["A phospholipid activator","Tissue factor","Plasmin","Collagen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The prothrombin time is the time for a fibrin clot to form in a lab tube when tissue factor is added to the patient's plasma. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man is evaluated for a suspected coagulation disorder. Which test is NOT among the initial screening tests?","choices":["Fibrinogen level","Activated partial thromboplastin time","Prothrombin time","D-dimer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The initial screens are the platelet count, size and shape, bleeding time, partial thromboplastin time, prothrombin time and fibrinogen level; D-dimer belongs to the thrombotic workup. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a clot that formed inside his femoral vein and partly blocks flow. Which term names a clot formed in situ within the vascular system?","choices":["Platelet plug","Fibrin degradation product","D-dimer","Thrombus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A thrombus is a blood clot formed in situ within the vascular system of the body that impedes blood flow. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a prolonged partial thromboplastin time and a normal platelet count. Which phase of hemostasis is most likely affected?","choices":["Primary hemostasis","Fibrinolysis","Secondary hemostasis","Vasoconstriction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The partial thromboplastin time tests the intrinsic and common coagulation pathways, so a prolonged result points to the clotting factors, secondary hemostasis. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a prolonged prothrombin time, with a normal partial thromboplastin time, fibrinogen, D-dimer and platelet count. Which cause fits?","choices":["Hemophilia A","Acute thrombosis","Immune thrombocytopenia","A vitamin K antagonist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An isolated prolonged prothrombin time with everything else normal fits liver disease, a vitamin K antagonist, factor VII deficiency or an oral factor Xa inhibitor. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has a prolonged thrombin time. Which pair of causes prolongs it?","choices":["Warfarin and vitamin K deficiency","Hemophilia A and B","Heparin and dabigatran","Factor VII and factor XII deficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heparin and dabigatran prolong the thrombin time along with the activated partial thromboplastin time. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with sepsis is evaluated for disseminated intravascular coagulation. Which finding is decreased?","choices":["Fibrin degradation products","Prothrombin time","Activated partial thromboplastin time","Protein C, antithrombin and protein S"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In disseminated intravascular coagulation the protease inhibitors, including protein C, antithrombin and protein S, are decreased. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has an isolated prolonged partial thromboplastin time that does not correct when mixed one to one with normal plasma. What does this suggest?","choices":["Factor VIII deficiency","An inhibitor","Factor IX deficiency","Vitamin K deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When a mixing study does not correct, an inhibitor is present, such as a specific factor inhibitor or lupus anticoagulant. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has thrombocytopenia, and his smear shows inclusions inside neutrophils. Which disorder fits?","choices":["Gray platelet syndrome","May-Hegglin anomaly","Immune thrombocytopenia","Hemophilia B"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophil inclusions on the smear point to May-Hegglin anomaly, a myosin heavy chain 9 disorder, among the morphologic causes of platelet abnormality. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman bleeds easily. Her platelet count, prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time are normal, and platelet function testing is abnormal. Which history best explains this?","choices":["A diet very low in vitamin K","A family history of hemophilia","Regular aspirin or nonsteroidal drug use","Long-term heparin therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin and other nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs give a normal count, prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time with abnormal platelet function, and patients may not realize an over-the-counter product contains them. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has a bedside bleeding time after a standardized forearm puncture. What is being timed?","choices":["How long plasma takes to clot with tissue factor","How long the puncture keeps bleeding","How long plasma takes to clot with a phospholipid activator","How long a formed clot takes to dissolve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bleeding time is the time in minutes for a standardized, superficial skin puncture to stop bleeding, and it is performed at the bedside. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman bleeds easily. Her platelet count, prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time are all normal. Which should be investigated next?","choices":["Platelet function","Intrinsic pathway factors","Extrinsic pathway factors","Common pathway factors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Normal prothrombin time and partial thromboplastin time with bleeding suggest platelet dysfunction, so platelet function testing and von Willebrand studies come next. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with joint bleeding has a normal prothrombin time and an activated partial thromboplastin time of 62 seconds (reference 21 to 35). Which deficiency fits?","choices":["Factor VII","Factor XIII","Factor VIII","Fibrinogen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time with a normal prothrombin time points to the intrinsic pathway; factor VIII deficiency is hemophilia A. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man cuts his finger and a platelet plug forms at the injury within minutes. Which term names this step?","choices":["Secondary hemostasis","Primary hemostasis","Fibrinolysis","Thrombus formation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Primary hemostasis is the formation of a platelet plug at the site of vascular injury; von Willebrand factor and glycoprotein IIb/IIIa carry it out. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 6"}]};
